  doi = {10.1590/es.305814},
  url = {http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0101-73302026000100612&lang=pt},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This article analyzes a still underexplored field in education: the struggles of social movements for digital sovereignty and their contributions to uncovering and politicizing the technosolutionist logics in education. Through network ethnography and critical discourse analysis, it examines the conceptions, propositions, and initiatives for digital sovereignty of three networks, articulated by the National Forum for the Democratization of Communication, the Coalition for Rights on the Web, and the Movement of the Homeless Workers. Based on a critical bibliography of historical-dialectical materialism, it was concluded that the political-pedagogical practices of these movements offer forms of resistance and contribute to raising awareness about the logics of technological solutionism in education.},
