@article{sheripotter2014opportunities,
  title = {Opportunities {{Abound}}: {{A Call}} for {{Leadership}} in the {{Life Sciences}}},
  author = {{Sheri Potter} and {Susan G. Stafford} and {Joseph L. Travis} and {James P. Collins} and {Steward T. A. Pickett} and {Charles B. Fenster} and {Eric S. Nagy} and {Muriel Poston}},
  date = {2014-12-12},
  journaltitle = {BioScience},
  shortjournal = {BioScience},
  volume = {65},
  number = {1},
  pages = {14--20},
  doi = {10.1093/biosci/biu202},
  url = {https://openalex.org/W2100760072},
  abstract = {The questions asked by scientists today are increasingly large and unbounded by geographic, cultural, or disciplinary constraints. How we do our science, how we organize as scientists to share research and solve problems, and how we transfer knowledge to society to help resolve complex questions are fundamentally changing. These shifts are—or should be—driving transformations in practice, in the way we work as scholars, educators, advocates, and communicators. But how? One of us (JPC, a past president of AIBS) has observed that “people who think about complex biological systems realize that they are full of multifaceted, evolved, and unscripted challenges.” In his presentation to the attendees of the 6 December 2013 AIBS Leadership in Biology event, he went on to highlight the complexity of the transformations that need to take place: “Given the complexity of what is happening right now in education and research, we have to consider how we advance everything more or less simultaneously, because working on only one facet—let's say, having the greatest undergraduate research program in the country—is not going to be helpful if those undergrads go into a class in introductory biology or in genetics where they are going to be asked just to memorize facts. We can't expect to meet the collective needs of science by focusing on only one part of the larger picture.” Many in the community have significantly advanced the conversation about pieces of the challenge before us. What outcomes do we (collectively) want to achieve through undergraduate, graduate, and K–12 education? What changes are required to the way we teach and communicate science to get us there (AAAS 2011, McBride et al. 2011, Allen-Ramdial and Campbell 2014)? What do scientific teams look like, and how do they function as they examine challenges of such complex scope that new ways to organize are required (Schmidt et al. 2012, American Academy of Arts and Sciences 2013, Pennington et al. 2013)? What professional pathways exist for new professionals entering the biological sciences, and will those pathways ensure the robust professional community required to advance our science (McDade et al. 2011, Baker 2014, Miller and Feldman 2014)? How will the vast amounts of data, open science, and rapidly changing tools change the way scientists pursue research and education (Gilmore 2003, Kaye et al. 2009, Vision 2010)? How do we provide the public with the tools needed to understand the best science available to make informed decisions personally and as informed global citizens (Silvertown 2009, Whitmer et al. 2010)? Among the cacophony of voices, how do we provide decisionmakers with the best information available to inform policy (Alberts 2010, Likens 2010, Winterfeldt 2013)? The challenge lies in understanding that these pieces are interwoven, and none can be resolved without a full view of the complex social system in which these questions are asked. Concerns are being raised about the ability of the life sciences community to rise to the challenges and address society's needs through twenty-first-century biology. Alberts and colleagues (2014) are pessimistic: “By many measures, the biological and medical sciences are in a golden age. That fact, which we celebrate, makes it all the more difficult to acknowledge that the current system contains systemic flaws that are threatening its future.” What should the community of life scientists and allied areas be doing differently if we are to realize the optimistic—as opposed to the pessimistic—vision of our future? As part of an effort to fulfill AIBS's leadership role at this time of rapid change, we began an exploratory process to better understand how the changing dynamics of science and society might influence the future of research and education in the biological sciences. To better understand what life scientists should be thinking about to understand the nature and scope of the opportunities and challenges before us, we asked three questions: (1) In order for the life sciences to thrive, what opportunities are too great to miss? (2) What transformations in practice and infrastructure must take place to prepare biology to meet the needs of society? (3) What challenges must be overcome to ensure our success? The AIBS Board of Directors and staff thought broadly about the individuals whom they have observed within and around the scientific professional community; purposively—but not systematically—identifying individuals who would hold a unique and informed view about the big picture of the changes taking place in science and society (see www.socialresearchmethods.net/kb/sampnon.php). They then asked the three questions above through an exploratory interview process that included one-on-one conversations with 30 entrepreneurs, thought leaders, change agents, funders, and others identified for their distinctive perspectives and depth of knowledge about how science and society are changing and how the life sciences, therefore, might be expected to change in order to keep pace and prepare for the future. The interviewed professionals had vastly different professional and personal backgrounds, many having held more than one key leadership position in the field at government agencies and nonprofits and in industry and academia; others held leadership positions in other fields; and others still engaged in truly entrepreneurial approaches to making advances against seemingly intractable issues at the science–society interface. Each interviewer was provided with a protocol that prescribed how to approach the conversation. The primary goal was to listen. The interviewers were charged with taking detailed notes that captured the observations of the interviewees, with the least bias possible. Because the interview questions were intentionally broad and the experiences of the interviewees were diverse, the goal was not to quantify results and present a compelling argument for or against a claim. Instead, the goal was to listen and gain a richer understanding of how a diverse group of individuals viewed our current professional environment and to use that collective body of opinions as a window of insight that might initiate a new dialogue about the future of the biological sciences. All of those interviewed were promised that their identities would be kept confidential unless they later gave permission to have statements attributed to them. Some interviewees were subsequently asked for permission in writing to be quoted by name in this article. All of the quotations that we cite in this article as personal communications derived from the interviews were approved by the source in writing, and permission was granted to use their names. One interviewee who agreed for a quotation to be used but preferred that his or her name be withheld is cited here as anonymous. The body of interviews was examined collectively and then presented in a framework that would promote discussion at the AIBS Leadership in Biology meeting and Council event, held in Washington, DC, on 6 December 2013. The initial synthesis revealed strong common threads that could be connected to examine the challenges from a systems perspective. These common threads were presented to the event attendees for discussion and synthesis using figure 1 as a facilitation tool. The attendees at the meeting included representatives from scholarly societies, government agencies, and funding agencies who all serve the biological sciences professional community. The stage for the day's discussions was set by an overview presentation that conveyed the depth and breadth of the opportunities and challenges made by a coauthor of this article (JPC). The other coauthors all had an active, participatory role in facilitating the day's conversations and in listening to the opinions expressed in small and large group settings. Facilitation image used to discuss common threads that emerged from the exploratory interviews at the 6 December 2013 Leadership in Biology event. The AIBS Leadership in Biology program is the latest in a series of steps that the organization has taken to focus its service to life scientists. Striving to inform decisions and to catalyze action around issues that affect the field broadly in this time of dynamic change (Potter et al. 2013), AIBS has redefined and positioned itself to be better equipped as the organization that is Bringing Biology to Life. Additional details about the changes that have taken place within AIBS can be found in an editorial written by one of this report's coauthors (Stafford 2014). The questions that society requires science to answer now and in the coming decades are increasingly complex, and at the same time, the tools of science are becoming increasingly accessible to the broader public. What were previously activities restricted to a few are now being enriched by significant citizen participation, even in the garages of science-interested citizens exploring their curiosities in a DIY—do it yourself—way. And that is happening not just in the United States. We live in an increasingly globalized world, with greater accessibility to some scientific tools and data than ever before. “The technological and cost barriers are dropping rapidly, which changes the innovation ecosystem of biology—who can play and what is possible” (David Rejeski, Wilson Center, Washington, DC, personal communication, 10 September 2013). Nearly every person interviewed by AIBS noted that the greatest challenges and opportunities of modern life science include the shift to cross-disciplinary science and the new requirements to manage the vast amounts of data being produced. There are many dimensions to these changes: Although the life sciences, broadly, are crucially important to answering the emerging scientific questions, there is a crucial need for “respect for a multidisciplinary perspective. We need to take the time to listen to one another and see what each has to offer the other. We need everyone (across all sciences) working together” (Pamela Matson, Stanford University, Stanford, California, personal communication, 17 October 2013). There is a trend toward “big science”—a significant force in how we organize and collaborate to answer questions. Big data, transdisciplinary research, and biocomplexity are crucial areas for investment, but nearly all of the respondents cautioned that investment in the new should not be undertaken at the cost of continuing traditional disciplinary research. “The slow pace of accumulation of essential natural history knowledge for many economically important species, from fisheries to crop pests, has repeatedly hindered the development of robust, predictive policies that would benefit humanity. In many industries, this has resulted in repeated failures of sustainable management, even though these extractive systems are the very ones for which natural history knowledge should be most complete” (Tewksbury et al. 2014). There is a changing intellectual property structure in the life sciences. It is still the case that each institution has its own rules for collaboration, on intellectual property, and concerning the right to claim something as one's own. Competitiveness can potentially squelch sharing, which may inhibit collaboration. The old model was, for example, to develop a drug and then “patent the hell out of it” (David Rejeski, Wilson Center, Washington, DC, personal communication, 10 September 2013). The new—and potentially more collaborative—model involves open-source systems using repositories of standardized biological parts that can be readily accessed and shared (Sonny Ramaswamy, National Institute of Food and Agriculture, Washington, DC, personal communication, 25 September 2013). A significant investment is being made by the National Science Foundation (NSF) in cyberinfrastructure. The Cyberinfrastructure Framework for 21st Century Science and Engineering (NSF 2012) is a funding mechanism designed to “provide a comprehensive, integrated, sustainable, and secure cyberinfrastructure to accelerate research and education and new functional capabilities in computational and data-intensive science and engineering, thereby transforming our ability to effectively address and solve the many complex problems facing science and society.” A vision and strategy for that infrastructure was outlined in 2012 and describes for the complexity of the task, including meeting increased storage demands, long-term sustainability, curation, and providing new analysis that will drive discovery (NSF 2012). Great advances in the life and geosciences have been made in developing a cyberinfrastructure that meets the interoperability and integration requirements considered necessary for this global data currency. “Finding solutions to these last two pieces is the holy grail in unlocking the challenge,” commented M. Lee Allison, state geologist of Arizona and principal investigator for the NSF-funded Test Enterprise Governance project for the EarthCube, an initiative to create a community-driven knowledge and data management system for the geosciences. His team also codirects an international planning effort for global e-infrastructure and data management. Allison likens the emerging cyberinfrastructure to the data version of the World Wide Web and says that the research community is well on its way to having that capacity up and running, noting that “it's within our reach that a global cyberinfrastructure will be accessible and usable for the 90\% of individual scientific investigators who constitute the long tail of scientists who don't need or use supercomputers” (M. Lee Allison, state geologist of Arizona, Tucson, Arizona, personal communication, 18 July 2014). Beyond the infrastructure itself, expressed by a science at highlight the that such an infrastructure has broad for how we approach scientific and are going to need new to We will need to se